[32m+[m[32m## AERIS[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m![AERIS process cross-section. Particulate-laden flue gas enters from the left, passes a high-voltage ionization electrode, and particles migrate onto two collector plates. A motor-driven rapping assembly dislodges the captured material into a sealed soot collection hopper; cleaner gas exits right. Three cards below describe the autonomy layers: power-assisted, maintenance-autonomous and data-autonomous.](assets/aeris.svg)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Retrofitting industrial chimneys into autonomous pollution-control systems.**[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mConventional electrostatic precipitators are large, fixed, grid-dependent installations that need manual cleaning and offer limited continuous visibility — which puts them out of reach of smaller industrial units. AERIS is designed as a retrofit for the equipment those units already have: brick kilns, foundries, small manufacturing and process industries.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThree layers of autonomy:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Power-assisted** — solar, waste-heat thermoelectric generation and kinetic harvesting, to reduce auxiliary power demand.[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Maintenance-autonomous** — a motor and blade rapping assembly dislodges collected particulate, reducing manual cleaning intervention.[m
[32m+[m[32m- **Data-autonomous** — an ESP32 with PM2.5 and MQ-135 sensing publishes continuous telemetry to a dashboard.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe early prototype used a TV flyback-derived high-voltage stage (~25 kV) driving an ionization and collector chamber, with a vibration motor for cleaning and a sealed bin for collected soot. **Early prototype demonstrated using simulated particulate smoke.** The current build adds an externally mounted TEG module for waste-heat recovery and a blade rapping assembly.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Status:** prototype build in progress. The next milestone is controlled laboratory validation of collection efficiency, power consumption and cleaning effectiveness. No performance, compliance or deployment claims are made until that testing exists.[m
