[32m+[m[32m## Meeting Copilot[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m![Meeting Copilot signal-flow diagram. Meeting audio is processed on the local device by Whisper speech recognition with context and memory. A dashed boundary separates the local device from the network, beyond which a thin relay passes requests to an LLM provider — Ollama, Groq, OpenAI or Anthropic. Outputs are live transcript, suggestion, meeting memory and summary.](assets/meeting-copilot.svg)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Meeting software should do more than record what happened.**[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mA Windows desktop client that transcribes locally with Whisper and helps you respond while the meeting is still running — on a hotkey, or by detecting that someone has put a question to you. Transcripts and meeting history stay on disk, and past meetings can be pulled in as context for the next one.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Architecture.** Audio capture, transcription and history are local. Only the model call crosses the network, and it goes through a thin backend relay so that no privileged API key ever ships inside a distributed desktop client. Providers are pluggable: Ollama for fully local inference, or Groq, OpenAI and Anthropic.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Direction.** Exploring task-specific models for meeting understanding rather than routing everything through a general-purpose LLM — a bet on latency, cost and control. Not a claim that it beats a frontier model today.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Status:** private beta with an open waitlist. The desktop client is closed-source; this one is intended to become a product.[m
